;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The eqz test's context step, by composing the dispatcher's phases
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The step of the halt body ((const V) (testop eqz) ...) is proved by
; abstracting each phase of the dispatcher as its own fact and composing:
;
;   dteq-slice      the pure testop rule on the slice (const V, testop);
;   dteq-prefix7    prefix7 of the body is 1 (val-p of the const; the testop
;                   stops it);
;   dteq-ctxt-abs   the context step with prefix7's value as a HYPOTHESIS
;                   (the definition instance's prefix7 term then matches the
;                   hypothesis instead of unfolding), instantiated as
;                   dteq-ctxt by dteq-prefix7;
;   dteq-s141-8/9   the return-label pure rule's search is nil at the two
;                   positions it tries;
;   dteq-spure      step_pure of the whole body is nil;
;   dteq-split      the read split of the config is nil;
;   dteq-step-abs   step = the ctxt result, from the three phase facts (pure,
;                   read, ctxt) through the step / r-pure / r-read definition
;                   instances; dteq-step instantiates it;
;   dteq-label      the halt block's label 0 (ctxt-label + dteq-step);
;   dteq-labelctxt  the context step at function 6's label 1 (label 0 at the
;                   head, the dispatch's rest after it);
;   dteq-label6     function 6's label 1;
;   dteq-outer      frame 6 through dstep-frame-outer-gen.
;
; THE THEORY LESSON, measured the hard way: do NOT disable val-p.  Concrete
; instructions' val-p checks (labels, frames, testops) must evaluate by their
; tags, or every search case-splits on them and the spurious branches stall.
; Keep $truncz and truncate DISABLED instead: then the const's val-p goes
; val-p -> num_-p -> in-p -> un-p -> truncz-value -> T, using the un-p sp 32
; hypothesis.  The value's bounds (integerp, 0 <= V < 2^32) are carried too,
